CHAPTER_SUMMARIES = [
"Psychopathology is not understood through symptom matching alone. A defensible formulation combines clinical interview, mental status examination, collateral information, standardised measures, and behavioural observation; considers normal variation, temporary stress, medical conditions, and substance effects; and relates developmental, biological, learning, cognitive, and emotional factors to the person’s context.",
"Reliable diagnostic reasoning integrates categorical, dimensional, developmental, and contextual information. Assessment draws on structured and semi-structured interviews, screening and severity measures, risk assessment, and cultural formulation; compares alternatives—including primary and secondary symptoms, trait and state patterns, psychosis and dissociation, and mania and substance activation—and accounts for base rates, measurement error, clinician judgement, cultural interpretation, and informant differences.",
"Neurodevelopmental formulations require developmental history and cross-setting evidence from caregivers, school records, adaptive functioning, cognition, and achievement. Differential reasoning includes language difference and language disorder, ADHD and anxiety, autism and social anxiety, and learning disorder and inadequate instruction; interpretation also considers genetic variation, prenatal and perinatal influences, neurodevelopmental pathways, language development, and executive function.",
"Assessment of psychotic phenomena requires attention to perception, beliefs, thought organisation, motivation, behaviour, and reality testing. Mental-status findings and a symptom timeline should be interpreted alongside substance history, physical and neurological review, and collateral information; differential diagnoses include mood-related and substance-induced psychosis and delirium. The chapter also considers polygenic risk, neurodevelopmental vulnerability, dopaminergic signalling, cognitive biases, and stress sensitivity.",
"Bipolar and related disorders are considered through longitudinal patterns in mood, energy, activity, sleep, cognition, and behaviour, including manic or hypomanic states and depressive episodes. Assessment uses mood and sleep/activity timelines, collateral history, medication and substance review, and family history; alternatives include unipolar depression, ADHD, borderline personality features, and substance-induced mood change. Contributors discussed include familial liability, circadian disruption, reward sensitivity, stressful events, and sleep loss.",
"Depressive presentations require evaluation of mood, interest, motivation, cognition, bodily function, and self-evaluation, with attention to grief and medical illness. The assessment framework combines a depression interview, suicide-risk assessment, bipolar screening, medical review, and grief history; it distinguishes normal sadness, bereavement, bipolar depression, and substance-induced depression, while considering genetic vulnerability, stress exposure, cognitive patterns, behavioural withdrawal, inflammation, and physical illness.",
"Anxiety disorders are considered in terms of fear, anticipatory threat, physiological arousal, and avoidance when these affect functioning. Assessment includes fear and avoidance hierarchies, panic and worry histories, medical review, and substance and caffeine history; differential considerations include cardiac or endocrine conditions, substance effects, OCD, and PTSD. The chapter discusses temperamental inhibition, learning and conditioning, threat appraisal, intolerance of uncertainty, and interoceptive sensitivity.",
"Obsessive-compulsive and related presentations include intrusive thoughts, urges, or images; repetitive behaviours; preoccupations; and body-focused patterns associated with distress reduction or rigid threat beliefs. Assessment includes an obsession–compulsion interview, functional analysis of rituals, insight assessment, family accommodation, and skin and hair health review; differential considerations include generalised worry, psychotic delusions, autism-related routines, and eating-disorder body concerns. The chapter discusses habit learning, negative reinforcement, inflated responsibility, thought–action fusion, and perfectionism.",
"Trauma- and stressor-related presentations are considered in relation to traumatic events, severe stressors, attachment disruption, loss, and major life changes, interpreted within developmental and cultural contexts. Assessment includes a trauma-informed interview, event and symptom timeline, dissociation assessment, risk assessment, and sleep review; alternatives include panic disorder, OCD, psychosis, and dissociative disorders. The chapter discusses trauma exposure, peritraumatic responses, prior adversity, appraisal, and memory processes.",
"Dissociative and somatic symptom presentations require integration of memory, identity, consciousness, bodily experience, symptom attention, and health-related behaviour with careful medical and psychological assessment. The chapter brings together medical evaluation, neurological review, dissociation interview, symptom timeline, and health-care-use history; alternatives include epilepsy, delirium, neurological disease, and substance effects. Trauma history, attention to bodily sensations, illness beliefs, stress physiology, and learning processes are also considered.",
"Eating, sleep, and elimination disorders require assessment of physiological, behavioural, developmental, cultural, and psychological factors. The chapter combines medical and nutritional assessment, eating-disorder interview, weight and growth history, sleep diary, and sleep-medicine referral; differential considerations include medical weight loss, gastrointestinal illness, OCD rituals, and depression-related appetite change. It also discusses genetic vulnerability, reward and habit processes, body-image pressures, dieting and restriction, and sensory sensitivity.",
"Substance-related and addictive disorders are considered through patterns of use, reinforcement, neuroadaptation, social context, availability, coping, and co-occurring health conditions. Assessment integrates a substance-use timeline, quantity–frequency pattern, withdrawal and overdose history, and motivational assessment; alternatives include recreational use without disorder, medically supervised physiological dependence, bipolar activation, and ADHD symptoms. The chapter discusses reward learning, negative reinforcement, neuroadaptation, trauma and stress, and peer context.",
"Personality functioning is assessed through enduring patterns of self, relationships, emotion, cognition, and behaviour when these patterns are inflexible, pervasive, and associated with distress or impairment. The chapter emphasises longitudinal and cross-situational history, functional impact, trait measures, and collateral information; differential considerations include bipolar disorder, PTSD, autism spectrum disorder, and ADHD. Contributors discussed include temperament, attachment and relational learning, developmental adversity, social learning, and emotion regulation.",
"Neurocognitive assessment addresses acquired changes in attention, memory, executive function, language, perception, social cognition, and daily functioning, integrating medical, neurological, psychological, and social information. The chapter discusses cognitive testing, screening, neuropsychological evaluation, functional assessment, and medical work-up; differential considerations include depression-related cognitive difficulty, psychosis, developmental intellectual disability, and substance intoxication or withdrawal. Contributors include neurodegeneration, vascular disease, infection, metabolic disturbance, and medication effects.",
"Clinical assessment of sexual functioning, gender-related distress, and atypical sexual interests centres consent, distress, impairment, development, culture, and risk. The chapter integrates sexual and relationship history, medical and medication review, consent and safeguarding assessment, and a respectful gender-affirming interview; alternatives include normal variation in desire, relationship dissatisfaction, medical pain conditions, and medication side effects. Medical conditions, medication effects, relationship factors, anxiety, and trauma history are also considered.",
"Integrative case formulation brings together multiple diagnoses, dimensional symptoms, risk, physical health, culture, development, and uncertainty. Assessment includes comprehensive risk assessment, physical-health review, medication reconciliation, collateral information, and cross-cutting symptom measures; alternatives include medical causes, diagnostic duplication, medication effects, and substance-induced syndromes. The chapter emphasises chronology, impairment, context, physical health, culture, development, and response to care.",
]
